WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.070
Our agent set a reminder to

2
00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.750
tisums the library.

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.240
Tisums!

4
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.180
Nothing stopped it, because

5
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.380
nothing was checking.

6
00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.090
What if another part of the

7
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.790
agent checked its work?

8
00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:18.590
Today: splitting an agent into

9
00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.250
roles that check each other.

10
00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.210
Big agent systems are

11
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:24.510
often split into roles.

12
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.490
A planner proposes each step.

13
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.620
A checker reviews it before anything runs.

14
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.140
Then a runner runs the tool, and a

15
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.580
person approves the actions.

16
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.690
In the team lab, the checker

17
00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.330
is five rules in code.

18
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.500
The tool must be on.

19
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.340
Every detail needs the right shape.

20
00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.130
Every name and number must come

21
00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.030
from the request or a result.

22
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.720
No earlier step may have failed, and a

23
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.120
person must say yes to any action.

24
00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.850
Nine Z copied as nine C: stopped,

25
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.410
because nobody said nine C.

26
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.590
Twenty-five o’clock: stopped, wrong shape.

27
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.220
The Saturday reminder: stopped,

28
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.200
because the step before it failed.

29
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.360
Why not make the checker a

30
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.600
second language model?

31
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.930
It could catch subtler slips,

32
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:20.670
like a rude message.

33
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.420
But it can be wrong too, and

34
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.580
fooled like the planner.

35
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.620
Rules never guess: start with them, and

36
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.000
add a model only where judgement is

37
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:31.280
needed.

38
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.370
More roles also cost more

39
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.550
tokens, time and money.

40
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.620
And errors multiply.

41
00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.030
If each of two steps is right ninety

42
00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.770
percent of the time, both are right only

43
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.270
eighty-one percent of the time.

44
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:47.980
Quick check!

45
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.970
The planner proposes the timetable for

46
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.270
nine C, but the request said nine Z.

47
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.510
What should the checker do?

48
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.430
Stop it.

49
00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.760
Nine C appears nowhere in the request or

50
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.560
the results, so it was copied wrongly.

51
00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.790
The checker doesn’t guess a fix:

52
00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:09.750
it hands the request back.

53
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.730
Darsi Tiny never learned to take a

54
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.470
checker’s feedback, so a person asks

55
00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:15.850
again.

56
00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.190
Here’s one rule as code: every word

57
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.770
in a call’s details must appear in

58
00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.310
the request or a result.

59
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.150
In this lesson’s code, you’ll write

60
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.870
a checker with three rules.

61
00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.290
In the app, send the Kafi weather

62
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.050
request: the planner proposes, the

63
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.610
checker passes it, the runner runs it.

64
00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:41.230
Then send the tidy reminder and nine Z.

65
00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.050
Turn the checker off and send them

66
00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.630
again: what gets through?

67
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:47.680
Your turn.

68
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.140
An agent runs three steps, each

69
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.060
right ninety percent of the time.

70
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.560
How often are all three right?

71
00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.350
About seventy-three

72
00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.410
percent: errors multiply.

73
00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.310
Remember: split the agent into

74
00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.270
planner, checker, runner and person.

75
00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.050
Strict rules in code make a

76
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.190
good first checker.

77
00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:12.770
And a person approves actions

78
00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.750
and handles what’s stopped.

79
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.040
Try it yourself: take a task

80
00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.460
like booking a study room.

81
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.740
Draw the agent’s roles, and write five

82
00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.480
strict rules its checker could test in

83
00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:25.760
code.

84
00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.670
Then find one slip your rules would miss.
